<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Puerto Rico (United States)</h1><p class="st-meta">1898 CE – present · capital San Juan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Former Spanish colony ruled by the United States since 1898, a Commonwealth since 1952 whose people are US citizens without a vote in Congress.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>American troops landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanica">Guánica</a> in July 1898 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish-American War</a>, months after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> had granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> an autonomous charter. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1898">Treaty of Paris</a> ceded the island to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>, and military rule gave way to civil government under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foraker-act">Foraker Act</a> of 1900, with an appointed governor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jones-shafroth-act">Jones Act of 1917</a> made Puerto Ricans US citizens. American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> companies dominated the economy until the 1940s. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rican-nationalist-party">Nationalist Party</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-albizu-campos">Pedro Albizu Campos</a> demanded independence, and police killed protesters at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ponce-puerto-rico">Ponce</a> in 1937. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-munoz-marin">Luis Muñoz Marín</a> became the first elected governor in 1948, and in 1952 the island became the Estado Libre Asociado, or Commonwealth, with its own constitution. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-bootstrap">Operation Bootstrap</a> industrialised it, while hundreds of thousands migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> remains an unincorporated US territory. Repeated referendums have shown divisions between statehood, Commonwealth and independence. A debt crisis led Congress to impose a fiscal oversight board in 2016, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-maria">Hurricane Maria</a> devastated the island in 2017.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/puerto-rico-united-states.jpg" alt="Puerto Rico (United States)" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: Cerejota · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Puerto_Rico.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1898 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>San Juan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Luis Muñoz Marín</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism and Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish and English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Commonwealth of Puerto Rico, Porto Rico</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1962&amp;focus=puerto-rico-united-states" aria-label="Puerto Rico (United States) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2889 1493 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Puerto Rico (United States) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3209 1749L3209 1749M3209 1749L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3209 1749h0M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3209 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3209 1749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks San Juan. <a href="/atlas?year=1962&amp;focus=puerto-rico-united-states">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
